PR #92  overall: potential_risk
Spec: Non-monotonic MDCFG table entries can break IOPMP memory-domain address matching, letting I/O devices escape their assigned memory access restrictions.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_domain.c:300-323  is_region_before
      Ordering predicate sorts by firmware flag, then ascending order (size), then ascending base. This is correct for PMP/ePMP first-match but is NOT top-address-ascending as MDCFG requires (MDCFG[m].t >= MDCFG[m-1].t). If an IOPMP driver naively reused this sort to build MDCFG entries, the resulting table would be non-monotonic. The invariant 'one sort order works for all protection mechanisms' is implicit and false for IOPMP — no abstraction layer translates domain-region ordering to the per-mechanism ordering each hardware type demands.
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_domain.c:385-495  sanitize_domain
      Correctly sorts regions via is_region_before then removes subset-covered regions with identical flags. The sort+dedup pass is well-structured: bubble-sort (lines 433-443) followed by linear subset-removal (lines 446-465). Input validation (possible HARTs mask, region validity, fw_region_inited) is thorough. For its current purpose (PMP/ePMP region table preparation), the ordering and dedup are deterministic and safe. No MDCFG violation exists because MDCFG is not implemented.
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_domain.c:220-271  sbi_domain_check_addr
      First-match linear scan over domain region table. Relies on sanitize_domain having established a deterministic total order — which is guaranteed because sanitize_domain is called during domain registration (via sbi_domain_register) and after root_add_memregion. The MMIO flag check at line 264 correctly prevents MMIO regions from satisfying non-MMIO access requests. The fallback at line 270 (M-mode always passes if no region matches) is an intentional design choice for M-mode software. No TOCTOU window here because region table is static after domain_finalize sets the flag.
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_hart_pmp.c:121-218  sbi_hart_smepmp_configure
      Programs PMP entries in domain region order (index = priority). Two-pass strategy: M-only regions first (with RLB set, lines 139-180), then MML set, then shared/SU-only regions (lines 187-206). Properly skips reserved entry SBI_SMEPMP_RESV_ENTRY, tracks firmware PMP indices via bitmap, and issues sbi_hart_pmp_fence() at line 216. The comment at line 213-214 ('Keep the RLB bit so that dynamic mappings can be done') is an intentional security trade-off for runtime flexibility. No MDCFG programming exists here — this is purely PMP.
  [OK] include/sbi/sbi_hart_protection.h:16-39  struct sbi_hart_protection
      Clean abstraction: configure/unconfigure callbacks are mandatory, map_range/unmap_range are optional, and rating-based selection (higher is better) determines the best mechanism. An IOPMP driver with rating > 200 would naturally supersede ePMP (rating 200). The struct design is sufficient for IOPMP registration — no spec violation exists in the interface definition itself. The gap is that no IOPMP driver exists to register here.
  [OK] include/sbi/sbi_domain.h:31-165  struct sbi_domain_memregion
      Well-defined structure with base, order (power-of-2 size), and flags including MMIO (bit 31) and FW (bit 30). Access permission flags (M_READABLE through SU_EXECUTABLE) are at bits 0-5 with SU_ACCESS_SHIFT at 3 for mode-agnostic checks. The SBI_DOMAIN_MEMREGION_MMIO flag correctly marks I/O regions. No MDCFG field exists because the structure models per-domain regions, not per-IOPMP-entry configuration — a separate IOPMP MDCFG entry structure would be needed, which is the design gap.
  [OK] platform/generic/sifive/sifive_dev_platform.c:17-42  sifive_smepmp_setup
      Configures MMIO regions as shared read/write with SBI_DOMAIN_MEMREGION_MMIO flag set. The comment 'Finer-grained access control is enforced by wgChecker rules' explicitly acknowledges that PMP/ePMP provides coarse I/O isolation and delegates fine-grained control to external hardware (WorldGuard). This is the exact role IOPMP would serve on non-SiFive platforms. The code is correct for its purpose; the MDCFG gap is in the missing IOPMP analogue of wgChecker, not in this setup function.
  [OK] docs/domain_support.md:1-10  N/A (documentation)
      Documentation correctly lists IOPMP alongside PMP, ePMP, and SiFive Shield as enforcement mechanisms. This documents architectural intent that IOPMP is a first-class mechanism, which is consistent with the draft PR's goal of adding MDCFG correction. No code violation exists; this is purely documentation of the design gap.
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_domain.c:699-756  root_add_memregion
      Adds a region to the root domain then re-sanitizes (sort + dedup) and iteratively merges consecutive same-permission/same-order adjacent regions. The do-while merge loop (lines 724-753) correctly handles chained merges. The compatibility check at line 713 prevents duplicate regions. This is the semantic equivalent of 'program MDCFG entries then auto-correct' — but only for PMP/ePMP ordering, not top-address ordering. The sanitize_domain call at line 726 uses is_region_before ordering, so the same ordering mismatch risk applies transitively.
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_domain.c:326-342  find_region
      First-match linear scan identical in structure to sbi_domain_check_addr but returns the region pointer instead of a boolean. Correctness depends on sorted region table (guaranteed by sanitize_domain). No independent validation of the ordering invariant. Safe for current code because the only callers are in paths where sanitize_domain has already run. The fallthrough-to-NULL behavior correctly signals 'no matching region' to callers.
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_hart_pmp.c:259-298  sbi_hart_oldpmp_configure
      Legacy PMP programming path. Same structural pattern as smepmp_configure: iterates domain regions in order, programs PMP entries with index = priority, disables remaining entries. Uses sbi_platform_pmp_set() for platform-specific PMP programming before the generic pmp_set(). The sbi_hart_pmp_fence() at line 296 ensures PMP changes are visible. Correct for legacy PMP; no MDCFG analogue exists here.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_domain_context.c:102-169  switch_to_next_domain_context
      Domain context switch: reassigns hart to target domain (lines 116-124), then unconfigure/configure hart protection (lines 127-128). Two concerns: (1) There is a window between unconfigure and configure where no protection is active — safe in practice because M-mode controls execution, but structurally fragile. (2) No hook exists for IOPMP context switching. If IOPMP were added, MDCFG reprogramming for the new domain would need to fire between unconfigure and configure, and the current code provides no extension point for this. The domain assignment update (lines 116-124) happens before protection reconfiguration, which is correct ordering (domain must reflect new owner before hardware is programmed).
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_hart_protection.c:21-42  sbi_hart_protection_register
